{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Autonomous Weapon Ethics.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Autonomous Weapon Ethics.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"autonomous weapon ethics autonomous weapon ethics","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Autonomous Weapon Ethics.md","fileName":"Autonomous Weapon Ethics.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Autonomous Weapon Ethics","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:09\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Machine Moral Status]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Machine Moral Status","Machine Moral Agency","aiethics.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Autonomous Weapon Ethics.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Autonomous Weapon Ethics.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"autonomous weapon ethics autonomous weapon ethics","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Autonomous Weapon Ethics.md","fileName":"Autonomous Weapon Ethics.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Autonomous Weapon Ethics","heading":"Autonomous Weapon Ethics","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Autonomous weapon ethics concerns systems that can select or attack targets with reduced immediate human control. The central questions include whether lethal judgment may be delegated, how distinction and proportionality can be maintained, and who is accountable when an autonomous action causes wrongful harm.\n\nThe high stakes make technical reliability only one part of the issue. A machine may execute constraints without bearing [[Machine Moral Agency]], leaving responsibility distributed among military authorities, designers, operators, and states. Proposals to prohibit such weapons reflect the view that some decisions should remain human even if automation is operationally effective.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Machine Moral Status","Machine Moral Agency","aiethics.epub